Subject: Genetic map construction in nested association mapping population
Message-ID: <waEQJoIlAlQ.zzTJx0n7aiIJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/waEQJoIlAlQ/m/zzTJx0n7aiIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Karl,
is it possible to construct a genetic map of a NAM double haploid population? I have 21 families sharing one common parent. Per family there are 50 offsprings. Is it better to calculate genetic map for each family separatly, even there are just 50 genotypes, or calculate over whole population(1050 genotypes, but weird allele frequencies(I guess)).

Thanks a lot for your suggestions.

Sincerely Tobias

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Mon Dec  1 16:46:16 2014
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Mon, 01 Dec 2014 16:46:16 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] Genetic map construction in nested association mapping population
Message-ID: <waEQJoIlAlQ.Xgy7xteJicEJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <waEQJoIlAlQ.zzTJx0n7aiIJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <waEQJoIlAlQ.zzTJx0n7aiIJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/waEQJoIlAlQ/m/Xgy7xteJicEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I think you should be able to pool them to estimate a common map, but I would also look at the family-specific maps, to see if there are any regions with big differences in recombination rates.

I would code each as A:B where A is the common parent and B is the other parent. In a particular family, if a marker is not segregating, I'd give all offspring missing genotypes.

karl

